  whatTitle: `What do you see?`,
  what1: `Imagine a black hole floating between you and the camera. The light traveling from you to the camera has to pass right by it, and gravity bends it. (The picture is mirrored, just like in a mirror.) The black spot in the middle is the black hole's <b>shadow</b>: light that should come from there has fallen in.`,
  what2: `Around the shadow you see yourself distorted: the closer to the shadow, the more strongly the light was bent. The ring of stars is light that was bent so strongly that it came from the side or even from behind the camera (in this simulation, the starry sky is there). And right next to the shadow you see yourself once more, in a thin ring: that light made a real hairpin turn (in this simulation, a mirror image of you also hangs behind the camera). Curious what the light did exactly? Have a look at the circles in the picture!`,
  what3: `A <b>spinning</b> black hole drags space around with it (a bit like a spoon turning in a jar of honey). Seen from the side (viewing angle 90°), the shadow therefore shifts sideways and becomes flatter on one side. Seen along the spin axis (viewing angle 0°), the shadow stays round, but the stars are most clearly dragged into a whirl.`,
  what4: `The bending of every light ray is calculated with the exact formulas of general relativity for a spinning (Kerr) black hole (Gralla &amp; Lupsasca, 2020).`,
  cancel: `Cancel`, close: `Close`, yourPhoto: `Your photo`,
  qrDialog: `QR code to download your photo`, qrAgainTitle: `Show recent QR codes again (key Q)`,
  // physics explanation points, raise a hand for a photo, photo service full
  pointsOn: `Explanation points in the picture (tap one: what does the light do here?)`,
  keepPoints: `Button to hide the explanation points (they come back by themselves after 2 minutes)`,
  pointsBtn: `Hide or show the explanation points`,
  pointsNA: `Explanation points do not work on this device.`,
  pt_weakT: `Bent just a little`,
  pt_weak: `This light came from behind the black hole (from you, in the camera picture) and passed it at a good distance. Gravity bent it only a little. In the same way, distant stars and galaxies appear slightly shifted in the sky. This was already measured in 1919, during a solar eclipse: one of the first confirmations of Einstein's theory!`,
  pt_n1T: `Light from beside and behind the camera`,
  pt_n1: `This light was bent so strongly that it did not come from in front of the camera, but from the side or even from behind it: it swung around the black hole. So here you are looking at what lies beside and behind the camera (in this simulation: the starry sky). That is how the broad ring of stars around the shadow comes about.`,
  pt_n1Bd: `This light was bent so strongly that it did not come from in front of the camera, but from the side or even from behind it: it swung around the black hole. So here you are looking at what lies beside and behind the camera (in this simulation: the chosen background picture).`,
  pt_n2T: `Once all the way round`,
  pt_n2: `This light went all the way around the black hole once before it reached the camera. So you see the camera picture here once more, in a very thin ring right next to the shadow. There are more such rings (twice, three times around…), but they are far too thin to see.`,
  pt_shadowT: `The shadow`,
  pt_shadow: `Light from you (behind the black hole) on its way to the camera comes too close to the black hole here and falls in. It never reaches the camera: that is why it is black here. The shadow is at least about 2.5 times as wide as the black hole itself (the event horizon).`,
  ptBent: `Bent here by about {d}°.`,
  handOn: `Raise a hand (no touching)`,
  handNoteCam: `Works when the camera is on.`,
  handNoteLoading: `Loading hand recognition…`,
  handNoteOk: `An open hand held up to the camera for a moment takes a photo (with the countdown if ticked). Works best up to 2–3 metres from the camera. While the settings are open no photo is taken; this note then says what the camera sees.`,
  handLive: `In view now: {hands} hand(s), {open} of them open.`,
  handNoteFail: `Hand recognition does not work on this device (or there was no internet the first time).`,
  handHint: `Raise your hand for a photo!`,
  serviceFull: `the photo service is full for today (free limit reached)`,
  // photo settings: group headings
  shotHow: `Taking a photo`,
  shotWhat: `With the photo`,
  shotNone: `No photos are taken while nothing above is ticked.`,
  // review of November 2026: scale and ET, the hairpin-turn ring, picture keeps dropping out
  what5: `How big is this black hole, actually? If it hung 1 meter from the camera (with the standard settings), it would be about the size of a golf ball, but as heavy as three to four Earths! Real black holes are at least a few times as heavy as the Sun. When two of them collide, they make ripples in space itself: <b>gravitational waves</b>. Telescopes let us <i>see</i> the universe; the <b>Einstein Telescope</b> will let us <i>hear</i> it, far across the cosmos.`,
  pt_uturnT: `Hairpin turn: light from straight behind the camera`,
  pt_uturn: `This light was bent by about 180°: it came from straight behind the camera, made a hairpin turn around the black hole and went back to the camera. In this simulation, a mirror image of you also hangs behind the camera. That is why you see yourself here once more, in a thin ring around the shadow.`,
  glLoop: `The picture keeps dropping out: this device cannot draw the black hole fast enough. The page will try again by itself in 5 minutes (or reload it now).`,
